  /* =========== 2c self-injection cards =========== */
  $('#nsInjCards').innerHTML=`
   <div class="card"><h4>Glatiramer acetate</h4><p>Subcutaneous only. The label lists the arms, abdomen, hips and thighs for self-injection and says to let the syringe reach room temperature for about 20 minutes. The 20 mg/mL (daily) and 40 mg/mL (three times weekly, at least 48 hours apart) products are not interchangeable. <sup class="c" data-ref="CopaxoneLabel2026" title="Source 95">95</sup></p><p>On 20 mg/mL, injection-site erythema occurred in 43% (placebo 10%). An immediate post-injection reaction (at least two of flushing, chest pain, palpitations, tachycardia, anxiety, dyspnoea, throat constriction or urticaria) occurred in about 16% (placebo 4%); it usually starts months into treatment, within seconds to minutes of an injection, and the label notes its symptoms may overlap with anaphylaxis. Lipoatrophy (about 2%) is probably permanent; correct technique and site rotation may reduce it. <sup class="c" data-ref="CopaxoneLabel2026" title="Source 95">95</sup></p></div>
   <div class="card"><h4>Interferon beta-1a (SC)</h4><p>The label titrates from 20% of the target dose over 4 weeks and suggests dosing on the same three days each week at least 48 hours apart, preferably late afternoon or evening. Analgesics or antipyretics on dosing days may ease flu-like symptoms. <sup class="c" data-ref="RebifLabel2025" title="Source 398">398</sup></p><p>Injection-site reactions occurred in 92% at 44 mcg (placebo 39%) and necrosis in 3%; influenza-like symptoms in 59% (51%). The label advises against injecting into an affected area until it has healed. <sup class="c" data-ref="RebifLabel2025" title="Source 398">398</sup></p></div>
   <div class="card"><h4>Ofatumumab</h4><p>Self-injected into the abdomen, thigh or outer upper arm, avoiding moles, scars, stretch marks and tender, bruised, red, scaly or hard skin. The label states that the first injection should be done under healthcare-professional guidance. <sup class="c" data-ref="KesimptaLabel2026" title="Source 239">239</sup></p><p>Systemic injection reactions occurred in 21% (15% with teriflunomide plus placebo injections), most after the first injection (14.4%), falling to 4.4% after the second and under 3% after the third. <sup class="c" data-ref="KesimptaLabel2026" title="Source 239">239</sup></p><div class="hook"><b>Exam hook</b>Ofatumumab systemic injection reactions: 14.4% at injection 1, 4.4% at injection 2.</div></div>
   <div class="card"><span class="ev early">Early evidence</span><h4>Injection anxiety</h4><p>In 101 people starting weekly IM interferon, injection self-efficacy before treatment predicted adherence at 6 months, through the ability to self-inject. <sup class="c" data-ref="Mohr2001" title="Source 318">318</sup> In a trial of 30 people unable to self-inject because of anxiety, 8 given a six-session nurse-administered programme self-injected at 6 weeks, against 3 controls (significant among completers, p=0.022; a trend by intention to treat, p=0.058). <sup class="c" data-ref="Mohr2005" title="Source 319">319</sup></p><div class="pt"><b>In plain words</b>Fear of needles is common and can stop treatment. In a small trial, nurse-led practice sessions helped some people inject themselves.</div></div>`;

